{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The heads of the tribes.</b> He gave honor to the princes by teaching them first, and afterward to all the children of Israel. And from where do we know that the rest of the matters were so? It is taught: ״וַיָּשֻׁבוּ אֵלָיו אַהֲרֹן וְכָל הַנְּשִׂיאִים בָּעֵדָה וַיְדַבֵּר מֹשֶׁה אֲלֵהֶם, וְאַחֲרֵי כֵן נִגְּשׁוּ כָּל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 34). And what did he see to say it here? It teaches that annulment of vows by a lay expert is valid, and if there is no lay expert, he annuls with three ordinary persons. Or can you say that Moses said this passage only to the princes? It is said here ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ and it is said regarding slaughter outside the Tabernacle (Leviticus 17) ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ — just as there it was said to Aaron and to his sons and to all the children of Israel, as it is said ״דַּבֵּר אֶל אַהֲרֹן״ etc., so too this was said to all of them (Sifrei; Nedarim 78a)." "<b>This is the word.</b> Moses prophesied with ״כֹּה אָמַר YHWH״ — ״at midnight״ (Exodus 11) — and the prophets prophesied with ״כֹּה אָמַר YHWH״; Moses was added over them, that he prophesied in the expression ״זֶה הַדָּבָר.״ Another interpretation — <b>This is the word</b> is a restrictive term, to say that a sage [releases] in the expression of release, and a husband [annuls] in the expression of annulment, as the expression of the verse here; and if they reversed them — it is not released and it is not annulled (Sifrei; Nedarim 77a)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:20.572318998Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}